module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.LinearGroupSchemes
public import WeilConjectures.AlgebraicGeometry.GroupScheme.GeneralLinear.ClosedSubgroup

/-! # The upper triangular group scheme

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Section 16a, printed pp.324--325.
(blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme)

The construction is the actual quotient of the coordinate ring of GL_n by the
below-diagonal coordinates. Its group structure is inherited from the quotient Hopf
algebra, and its inclusion is the spectrum of the quotient map.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.UpperTriangularGroup

variable (k : Type u) [Field k] (n : ℕ)

/-- The ideal generated by the coordinates strictly below the diagonal in k[GL_n].
Source: Milne, Section 16a, p.324 (blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme). -/
def ideal : Ideal (Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.CoordinateRing (LinearGroupSchemes.Index n) k) :=
  Ideal.span {a | ∃ i j : LinearGroupSchemes.Index n, j.down < i.down ∧
    a = Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.coord (LinearGroupSchemes.Index n) k i j}

/-- The below-diagonal ideal is a Hopf ideal.
Source: Milne, Section 16a, pp.324--325
(blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme).

Proof: the counit of each generator is zero. In the comultiplication
sum x_ir tensor x_rj for i > j, either i > r or r > j, so every summand
vanishes in the tensor product of the quotients. For the antipode, the
universal matrix over the quotient is upper triangular and invertible.
Its inverse is upper triangular over any commutative ring: its determinant
is the product of the diagonal entries, so each diagonal entry is a unit;
back substitution computes the upper triangular inverse. Thus the antipode
of every below-diagonal generator vanishes in the quotient as well.
The three conditions extend from the generators to the whole ideal. -/
@[instance] theorem ideal_isHopfIdeal : (ideal k n).IsHopfIdeal k := by
  sorry

/-- B_n = Spec(k[GL_n]/(x_ij : i > j)), with its quotient Hopf group structure.
Source: Milne, Section 16a, p.324 (blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme). -/
abbrev scheme : Over (Spec (.of k)) :=
  GeneralLinearGroup.subgroupScheme (ideal k n)

/-- The canonical closed group-scheme inclusion B_n into GL_n.
Source: Milne, Section 16a, p.324 (blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme). -/
abbrev inclusion : scheme k n ⟶ GeneralLinearGroup (LinearGroupSchemes.Index n) k :=
  GeneralLinearGroup.subgroupSchemeι (ideal k n)

/-- On every commutative test algebra, B_n consists exactly of upper triangular
invertible matrices, through its canonical inclusion.
Source: Milne, Section 16a, p.324 (blueprint: def-sfb-upper-triangular-group-scheme).

Proof: use GeneralLinearGroup.homMulEquiv to identify GL_n-points with
matrices. The quotient algebra universal property says that a point factors
uniquely through B_n precisely when all generators of the defining ideal
vanish. Evaluating x_ij gives the (i,j) matrix entry, so these are exactly
the below-diagonal vanishing equations, also over nonreduced algebras. -/
theorem points (A : Type u) [CommRing A] [Algebra k A]
    (g : Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup (LinearGroupSchemes.Index n) A) :
    (∃ x : (Spec (.of A)).asOver (Spec (.of k)) ⟶ scheme k n,
      GeneralLinearGroup.homMulEquiv (LinearGroupSchemes.Index n) k A
        (x ≫ inclusion k n) = g) ↔
    ∀ i j : LinearGroupSchemes.Index n, j.down < i.down → g.val i j = 0 := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.UpperTriangularGroup
